# systemd unit for `vike-strategy-builder` — the ONLY process in this workspace that runs `cargo`
# against source an authenticated caller supplies. See
# `crates/vike-strategy-builder/src/builder.rs` (the wire protocol, the auth domain, the scope
# argument — split out of `vike-strategy-plugin` by a controller ruling so a compiled plugin never
# inherits this service's dependency tree; that module's doc carries the full argument) and
# `docs/superpowers/specs/2026-09-21-runtime-loaded-rust-strategies-design.md`
# ("The builder service") for what this daemon does and why it is a SEPARATE service from
# `vike-backend backtest`, which only `dlopen`s this daemon's finished `.so` files off a shared
# filesystem — the two never talk to each other over a socket (see that design's "The builder
# gets its own authenticated loopback port" section).
#
# ⚠ EVERY PATH BELOW IS THE PLACEHOLDER PROJECT ROOT /srv/vike-<unit>. It is an example,
# not a requirement — substitute your own root everywhere, the same recipe every other unit in
# this directory documents:
#
#   R=/your/project/root
#   sed "s,/srv/vike-<unit>,$R,g" deploy/vike-strategy-builder.service > /tmp/vike-strategy-builder.service
#   grep -n /srv/vike-<unit> /tmp/vike-strategy-builder.service    # must print NOTHING
#   install -Dm644 /tmp/vike-strategy-builder.service ~/.config/systemd/user/vike-strategy-builder.service
#   systemctl --user daemon-reload && systemctl --user enable --now vike-strategy-builder
#
# (For a system-wide install use /etc/systemd/system/, add `User=` and `Group=`, change
# `WantedBy=default.target` to `WantedBy=multi-user.target` — this file ships the `--user`-friendly
# spelling, exactly as `deploy/vike-datahub.service` and `deploy/vike-tradehub.service` do; a SYSTEM
# manager has no `default.target` user session to pull it in — and drop the --user flags. Read the
# hardening block at the bottom first — under a --user manager the sandbox directives are applied
# as NO-OPS on a stock Ubuntu box, exactly as `deploy/vike-backtest.service`'s own header warns.)
#
# ⚠ **INSTALL THIS ONE AS A SYSTEM UNIT.** Every other daemon here only READS data; this one runs a
# caller's `build.rs`, so its filesystem jail, its credential and its address-family filter are
# what stand between that code and the rest of the box — and the one shape they were MEASURED
# under (prod, 2026-09-26, systemd 255) is the system manager. A `--user` install is a dev-box
# convenience, and on it assume none of the directives below the write grant are in force.
#
# ⚠ A SECOND, INDEPENDENT placeholder below — /checkout/vike-trader-rust — is NOT a sub-path of
# the project root above, and deliberately shares NO PREFIX with it: /srv/vike-<unit> is
# a prefix of a careless "/srv/vike-<unit>"-style placeholder, so the substitution
# above would rewrite this one to `$R-checkout` instead of leaving it a separate, independent
# substitution target. This placeholder is a tree holding crates/vike-model, crates/vike-strategy,
# crates/vike-indicators and crates/vike-strategy-plugin (plus the root Cargo.toml, for their
# `field.workspace = true` inheritance) — the vike-strategy-plugin cdylib template's own dependency
# closure — which a rendered plugin's Cargo.toml needs to resolve its `path` dependencies against.
#
# ⚠ **This does NOT have to be a second git checkout an operator maintains by hand, and until
# 2026-09-22 the only documented way to fill it was exactly that.** The workspace source ships as
# its own release asset now (`vike-strategy-source.tar.gz` — see
# `.github/workflows/release.yml`'s "Package the strategy builder's workspace source" step for the
# derived-closure argument and the size measurement behind shipping a subtree rather than the whole
# checkout), so the version this points at is pinned to the SAME release as the `bin/vike-backend`
# beside it, by construction rather than by an operator remembering to re-sync a checkout on every
# deploy. `scripts/fetch_release_tools.sh strategy-source [--project <root>]` downloads, verifies
# against `SHA256SUMS` and extracts it to `<project>/vendor/vike-strategy-source` — which is exactly
# the leaf this variable should then name. A real, hand-maintained checkout still works (cargo does
# not care how the tree arrived, only that `crates/vike-model` etc. are really there) and is a
# reasonable choice on a box that already keeps one for other reasons; the release asset is the
# path that keeps the two in step with no reason to remember.
#
# ⚠ **NEVER point this at a directory that IS, or CONTAINS, a real project root** — one with its
# own `settings/` holding the venue credential store. The jail below binds this variable's target
# back READ-ONLY into every compile, so a `settings/`-bearing tree here hands every caller's
# `build.rs` exactly the path this unit exists to hide. The binary itself refuses to start against
# such a tree (`builder::run`'s startup check), but the refusal is a backstop, not the plan — name
# a strategy-source-only tree (the release asset above, or a subtree of a hand-maintained checkout
# that carries no `settings/` of its own) in the first place.
#
# ⚠ **Wherever it lives, the JAIL below must re-expose it**, because the unit hides every
# directory a project root can live under and binds back only what this daemon needs. It is on the
# `BindReadOnlyPaths=` line as this same placeholder, so the substitution covers it — but a checkout
# under `/home` is not a supported answer (`ProtectHome=yes` hides the home, and this file carves no
# hole in it), which is one more reason the release asset under `<root>/vendor/` is the path to use.
#
# Include it in the SAME substitution pass, with its own `$CHECKOUT` — order does not matter now
# that the two placeholders share no prefix:
#
#   CHECKOUT=/path/to/your/vike-trader-rust     # or: <project root>/vendor/vike-strategy-source
#   sed "s,/checkout/vike-trader-rust,$CHECKOUT,g" ... (chain onto the sed above)
#   grep -n /checkout/vike-trader-rust /tmp/vike-strategy-builder.service    # must print NOTHING
#
# ⚠ THE RUST TOOLCHAIN THIS DAEMON COMPILES WITH LIVES INSIDE THE PROJECT — `$R/bin/rust-toolchain`
# — and is no longer a placeholder of its own. It used to be a THIRD independent placeholder
# (`/toolchain/rust`) pointing at the operator's `~/.rustup/toolchains/…/bin`, which is WHY this
# unit had to carry `ProtectHome=read-only` and open the operator's whole home directory. That was
# the security hole this file closes: the ONLY reason the builder read `/home` was to reach a
# compiler, and a compiler that compiles a caller's arbitrary source (Decision 2 below) reading the
# home that holds `~/.ssh`, a GitHub token and — via the account that owns them — the settings
# database of venue credentials, is a strictly wider grant than the job needs. Moving the toolchain
# into the project folder lets the home directory be hidden ENTIRELY (`ProtectHome=yes`, like every
# sibling unit), and follows the owner's standing ruling that a durable artifact on the box lives in
# the PROJECT FOLDER, not in `$HOME` or beside a scratch lane. It sits under `bin/` because it is a
# program the project RUNS but did not compile — the same rule (`vike_model::state_path::
# PROJECT_BIN_DIR`) that puts the JRE at `bin/jre/` and the LightGBM binary at `bin/lightgbm/`.
#
# ⚠ **The toolchain is PINNED to the release binary's own rustc, and the pin is CHECKED.** This
# daemon compiles a plugin that the RELEASE `vike-backend` (the backtest server) then `dlopen`s, and
# `vike_strategy_plugin::loader::load` refuses a plugin whose four-field toolchain fingerprint
# (`rustc=<release>+<commit-hash>;target;profile;opt`) differs from the loader's — so a builder on a
# DIFFERENT rustc than the release means every strategy a user writes compiles and is then refused
# at Run, silently, invisible to `docker build` and to every smoke. #2175 guards this for the
# CONTAINER image; this line is the same guard for the HOST builder. The pin is `1.97.1` — the
# `channel` in `rust-toolchain.toml` (measured on the deployed binary 2026-09-26: `rustc 1.97.1
# (8bab26f4f 2026-07-14)`), and `crates/vike-ops/tests/deploy_layout_gate.rs`'s
# `the_builder_toolchain_pin_matches_the_workspace_channel` holds THIS FILE's stamped version equal
# to that channel so a `rust-toolchain.toml` bump that forgets the builder reddens CI rather than
# refusing every user's plugin on the box. The commit-hash follows the version by construction: both
# the release binary and this toolchain install the SAME `1.97.1` from the SAME rustup dist.
# vike-strategy-builder-toolchain-pin: 1.97.1
#
# PROVISION IT ONCE (does NOT touch the operator's own `~/.rustup`, which other things on the box
# use — it installs a fresh minimal toolchain into a throwaway rustup home and MOVES the result into
# the project). `--profile minimal` is rustc + cargo + rust-std for the host target and nothing else
# (~0.6 GB), against ~1.7 GB for a full `cp -a` of an existing toolchain that also carries rustdoc,
# clippy, rustfmt and other-target stds this daemon never invokes:
#
#   R=/your/project/root
#   TMP_RUSTUP="$(mktemp -d)"
#   RUSTUP_HOME="$TMP_RUSTUP" CARGO_HOME="$(mktemp -d)" \
#     rustup toolchain install 1.97.1 --profile minimal --target x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu
#   install -d -m755 "$R/bin"
#   mv "$TMP_RUSTUP/toolchains/1.97.1-x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu" "$R/bin/rust-toolchain"
#   rm -rf "$TMP_RUSTUP"
#   "$R/bin/rust-toolchain/bin/rustc" -Vv | grep '^release:'    # must print `release: 1.97.1`
#
# (A `cp -a ~/.rustup/toolchains/1.97.1-x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu "$R/bin/rust-toolchain"` also works
# on a box that already has that exact version and no network, at the larger size above.)
#
# ⚠ THIS DAEMON SHIPS INSIDE THE MULTICALL NOW, NOT AS ITS OWN BINARY. `crates/vike/Cargo.toml`'s
# `vike-strategy-builder` feature (forwarded by `full`) carries this service as a `vike-backend
# strategy-builder` verb — the same join `trade`/`datahub`/`backtest` already went through, and for
# the identical reason: `.github/workflows/release.yml` builds `-p vike-backend --bin vike-backend
# --features full` and NOTHING ELSE, so a unit naming a separate `vike-strategy-builder` binary
# would ExecStart a file no release produces. The crate's OWN binary
# (`crates/vike-strategy-builder/src/bin/vike-strategy-builder.rs`) still exists and still builds —
# see `crates/vike-strategy-builder/src/builder.rs`'s `run`, which both composition roots call, for
# why folding this into the multicall does not touch `34b8d6756`'s split: that commit kept
# `tracing`/`vike-datahub-client` out of a COMPILED PLUGIN's link
# (`crates/vike-strategy-plugin`'s own tree), and this join is the DISPATCHER taking on this
# crate, which a plugin never sees either way.
#
# Building it yourself is the same one command every other daemon in this directory uses, under
# the multicall's own package — and the `--bin` follows the PACKAGE name, because
# crates/vike/Cargo.toml declares no `[[bin]]`:
#   cargo build --release -p vike-backend --bin vike-backend --features full
#   install -d -m755 /srv/vike-<unit>/bin
#   install -m755 target/release/vike-backend /srv/vike-<unit>/bin/vike-backend
#
# ⚠ It ALSO needs the workspace SOURCE TREE `VIKE_STRATEGY_BUILDER_WORKSPACE_ROOT` names below —
# see that variable's own note further down for where that comes from on a real deploy (a release
# asset, extracted beside the binaries — never a second git checkout an operator maintains by
# hand).
#
# ⚠ **INSTALLING THIS UNIT IS NOT THE SAME AS THIS DAEMON JOINING THE AUTOMATED DEPLOY PIPELINE —
# a SECOND, SEPARATE act is required, and it is easy to do the first and believe the second
# happened too.** `deploy/sbin/vike-trader-ci-deploy`'s `DEPLOY_UNITS` names this unit and carries
# a real `unit_shape` probe for it, so a green tag's automated `deploy` WILL pre-flight and restart
# it going forward — but ONLY once that script's INSTALLED copy at `/usr/local/sbin/` is refreshed.
# That script is installed OUT OF BAND, by hand (`docs/ops/upgrading.md`'s install step has the
# exact command), and nothing on this box does that for you. Until you run it, a green automated
# deploy keeps installing a fresh `bin/vike-backend` while never pre-flighting or restarting THIS
# unit — silently, because the installed helper's OLD `DEPLOY_UNITS` simply does not name it, and
# nothing on the box reports that as a problem (`verify-tools`/`helper_identity` catch a stale copy
# missing a TOOL or a VERB, not a stale `DEPLOY_UNITS` roster). Same shape as the incident that
# motivated `verify-tools` in the first place, applied to a roster instead of a tool table.
#
# ⚠ THE KEY ARRIVES AS A CREDENTIAL FILE, NEVER AS AN `Environment=` LINE — and never in this file.
# A tracked unit is the worst place to put a real secret, and the key is the ONE credential this
# service refuses to start without (`crates/vike-strategy-builder/src/builder.rs`'s `run`, called
# by both this crate's own binary and the multicall's `strategy-builder` verb). The operator keeps it
# in ONE file, `<root>/settings/strategy-builder.key` (mode 0600, owned by the service user), and the
# `LoadCredential=` line below has systemd — PID 1, as root, BEFORE this unit's sandbox exists —
# copy that file into the unit's private credential directory; `VIKE_STRATEGY_BUILDER_KEY_FILE`
# names the copy, and the binary reads the key out of it (`builder::resolve_keys`).
#
# ⚠ **Until 2026-09-26 this block told you to put the key in an `Environment=` drop-in, and that
# was the leak.** MEASURED on the production box: a unit's `Environment=` is readable by ANY local
# user, unprivileged, over D-Bus (`busctl get-property org.freedesktop.systemd1 <unit path>
# org.freedesktop.systemd1.Service Environment`) — and across all 243 loaded units on that box this
# key was the ONE secret-named variable exposed that way. The same value also sat in the daemon's
# own environment, which it handed to every `cargo` it spawned. `systemd.exec(5)` says it in so many
# words: environment variables "are not suitable for passing secrets … use LoadCredential=".
#
# MIGRATING a box that has the old drop-in: DELETE it (`/etc/systemd/system/<this unit>.d/*.conf`
# carrying `Environment=VIKE_STRATEGY_BUILDER_KEY=…`) in the same step as installing this file. With
# both in place the service REFUSES to start and names both variables — deliberately: two sources
# for one key is how a rotation changes the one the daemon is not reading.
#
# The key is also the file the Studio launcher reads over ssh (`scripts/studio.ps1`) to hand the GUI
# its build key, so this makes it ONE copy on the box where there used to be two (the drop-in and
# that file) that a rotation had to rewrite in step. To mint one, as the service user:
#   umask 077; head -c 32 /dev/urandom | od -An -tx1 | tr -d ' \n' > "$R/settings/strategy-builder.key"
#
# NEVER via `EnvironmentFile=<root>/.env` below: that file is operator TUNABLES, never a credential
# source, and `crates/vike-ops/tests/deploy_layout_gate.rs`'s
# `no_unit_makes_the_credential_store_an_environment_source` holds every unit in this directory to
# that rule already. This binary does not route through this workspace's credential store
# (`<project>/settings/secrets.env`, or the database under `settings/db` a migrated box answers
# from) the way the trading daemon does — see `crates/vike-strategy-builder/src/bin/
# vike-strategy-builder.rs`'s module doc for the scope note — and the builder key has no row there.
#
# ⚠ `VIKE_STRATEGY_BUILDER_WORKSPACE_ROOT` is NOT a secret (it names a directory, not a
# credential), so it DOES ship below as an ordinary `Environment=` line — unlike the key, an
# operator who forgets to substitute the checkout placeholder gets an immediate, loud refusal to
# start (`builder.rs`'s `run`) rather than a silently-wrong build tree. See
# `crates/vike-strategy-builder/src/render.rs`'s module doc for why there is no compile-time
# fallback here at all: a binary built in one checkout and deployed into another would otherwise
# silently point `cargo` at a tree that does not exist on this box.
#
# ⚠⚠ WHAT THIS DAEMON CAN SEE — A JAIL, NOT A DENYLIST. A caller's `build.rs` and proc-macros run as
# this unit's user with this unit's view of the filesystem, so that view is the whole security
# boundary around them. `ProtectSystem=strict` alone makes the rest of the filesystem READ-ONLY, not
# invisible, and MEASURED on the production box on 2026-09-26 (a build probe running this unit's own
# directives as a transient system unit) that left readable: the project's `settings/` (the
# credential database), `bin/ibkr-cpapi/.cpcreds` (a broker login, under `bin/` where no settings
# mask reaches), stray credential copies in a sibling checkout, and the CI runners' own
# `.credentials` files — every one of them a directory away from a project root. A denylist would
# have to name each of those and the next one; so instead the jail below mounts an EMPTY tmpfs over
# every top-level directory a project root or another project's data lives under, and binds back
# EXACTLY what this daemon needs:
#
#   read-only   <root>/bin/vike-backend              the binary this unit runs
#               <root>/bin/rust-toolchain            the compiler (and its cargo)
#               the source tree (the checkout placeholder)
#   read-write  <root>/bin/rust-toolchain/cargo-home cargo's registry cache and its lock
#               <root>/user_data/plugins             the artifacts this daemon exists to write
#
# Nothing else of the project root exists inside the unit — not `settings/`, not `bin/ibkr-cpapi`,
# not the trading node's state. Re-measured from inside the jail with a `find / -readable` sweep:
# the only paths left under the hidden trees were those five.
#
# ⚠ **YOUR ROOT'S TOP-LEVEL DIRECTORY MUST BE ON `TemporaryFileSystem=`.** The shipped list is `/mnt
# /srv /opt /media` — where a project root, a CI runner or another service's data lives on the boxes
# this was measured on. A root under `/data`, `/nvme` or `/var/lib/…` is NOT covered: add its
# top-level directory to that line (for `/var/lib/…` add the deepest directory that holds nothing
# this daemon needs), or the jail hides nothing around your root. `/home`, `/root` and `/run/user`
# are hidden separately, by `ProtectHome=yes`. `crates/vike-ops/tests/deploy_layout_gate.rs`'s
# `the_builder_reaches_only_what_it_builds` fails a PR whose unit's own root is not covered.
#
# ⚠ **PROVISION EVERY BOUND PATH BEFORE THE FIRST START.** A `BindPaths=`/`BindReadOnlyPaths=` source
# that does not exist fails the unit before the binary runs (`status=226/NAMESPACE`), and so does a
# missing credential (`status=243/CREDENTIALS`). As the service user, after the toolchain block
# above and the source-tree fetch:
#
#   install -d -m700 "$R/bin/rust-toolchain/cargo-home" "$R/user_data/plugins"
#   ls -l "$R/bin/vike-backend" "$R/bin/rust-toolchain/bin/cargo" "$CHECKOUT/Cargo.toml" \
#         "$R/settings/strategy-builder.key"     # all four must exist
#
# ⚠⚠ WHAT THIS UNIT DOES **NOT** CLOSE — the residuals, stated so nobody reads the jail as more than
# it is:
#
#   1. THE KEY FILE IS READABLE BY BUILD CODE. `$CREDENTIALS_DIRECTORY/builder-key` is readable by
#      this unit's user, and a `build.rs` runs AS that user, so it can read the service's own key.
#      Only a SEPARATE uid for the compile (cargo run as a second, key-less account) would hide it,
#      and that is not built. It is bounded, not open: the listener is loopback-only in CODE
#      (`builder::bind_addr`), and the code that could read the key is code whose author already
#      had to present that key to get it compiled. What the credential DID close is the key's reach
#      to every OTHER local user (D-Bus) and its presence in the environment cargo inherits.
#   2. NETWORK EGRESS STAYS OPEN. cargo downloads crates from crates.io, so `AF_INET`/`AF_INET6`
#      remain and a `build.rs` can open an outbound connection. (`AF_UNIX` is GONE — see
#      `RestrictAddressFamilies=` — so the D-Bus route to other units' `Environment=` is closed.)
#   3. OTHER SAME-USER PROCESSES' `/proc/<pid>/environ` IS READABLE. `ProtectProc=invisible` hides
#      processes of OTHER users; a daemon running as this unit's user stays visible, and its
#      environment readable, from inside the jail — MEASURED. `PrivatePIDs=` would close it and
#      does not exist before systemd 257 (the production box runs 255 and rejects it:
#      `Unknown assignment: PrivatePIDs=yes`). Reading another process's MEMORY is refused by the
#      kernel's `ptrace_scope=1` on that box — a host setting, not this file's. So: keep secrets out
#      of every sibling daemon's environment (measured for the trading daemon there: it carries no
#      secret-named variable; a sibling fed secrets through an `EnvironmentFile=` would not be
#      covered by that measurement).
#   4. `/etc` STAYS READABLE, because a compiler needs it (the resolver, the CA bundle, the password
#      database). The two secret-bearing `/etc` paths the sweep found are hidden by name
#      (`InaccessiblePaths=` below); anything a box adds under `/etc` later is not.
#   5. A CACHED ARTIFACT IS `dlopen`ed INSIDE THIS DAEMON on a cache hit (`render::build_plugin`'s
#      handshake check), so its initializers run with this process's environment and memory. Same
#      bound as (1): only this daemon writes that directory, and only for an authenticated caller.

[Unit]
Description=vike-strategy-builder — compiles an authenticated caller's Rust strategy into a cdylib
After=network-online.target
Wants=network-online.target

[Service]
Type=simple

# WorkingDirectory IS the project root, the same convention every unit in this directory follows.
# Inside the jail it is an EMPTY directory on the tmpfs that holds only the binds below — which is
# fine, because this daemon resolves nothing relative to it (every path it uses is named outright).
WorkingDirectory=/srv/vike-<unit>
# ⚠ The multicall verb, NOT the standalone binary — see the header note above. `strategy-builder`
# is `crates/vike/src/main.rs`'s `TOOLS` row name; it takes no argv (every knob below is an
# `Environment=` line), so there is nothing to append after it.
ExecStart=/srv/vike-<unit>/bin/vike-backend strategy-builder

# --- the listen address ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# ⚠ Loopback is not a configuration this binary can be talked OUT of — `builder::bind_addr` fixes
# the IP half in code, so unlike its sibling daemons there is no public-bind escape hatch to guard
# against here. Only the PORT is a real knob (avoiding a collision with vike-tradehub's 7879 or
# vike-backtest's 7880 on a box that runs more than one of these).
Environment=VIKE_STRATEGY_BUILDER_PORT=7881

# --- the key: a CREDENTIAL FILE, see the header ------------------------------------------------
# `LoadCredential=` is read by PID 1 as root in the HOST's view, before the jail exists — so its
# source can sit under `settings/`, which the service itself cannot see. `%d` is the unit's
# credential directory (`$CREDENTIALS_DIRECTORY`, `systemd.unit(5)`'s specifier table). The copy
# there is readable by the service user and root only (measured: mode 0440, owner root, plus an ACL
# naming the unit's `User=`). ⚠ The deploy pre-flight does NOT read it — it did, "through this same
# variable, as that user", until 2026-09-30, when that read was measured to be `Permission denied`
# from the mount namespace a CI runner deploys from. The pre-flight gives its probe a throwaway key
# of its own; `deploy/sbin/vike-trader-ci-deploy`'s `unit_shape` builder arm carries the measurement.
# ⚠ No `Environment=VIKE_STRATEGY_BUILDER_KEY=` line anywhere — here or in a drop-in. The service
# refuses to start with both forms set, and `crates/vike-ops/tests/deploy_layout_gate.rs`'s
# `the_builder_reaches_only_what_it_builds` refuses this file carrying one.
LoadCredential=builder-key:/srv/vike-<unit>/settings/strategy-builder.key
Environment=VIKE_STRATEGY_BUILDER_KEY_FILE=%d/builder-key

# --- where finished artifacts land, and how many this daemon keeps per strategy --------------
# ⚠⚠ **THIS DIRECTORY IS THE ENTIRE HAND-OFF, AND THE OTHER HALF OF IT IS IN
# `deploy/vike-backtest.service`.** The two daemons never talk over a socket; the compute daemon
# `dlopen`s the `.so` this one writes, and it finds that file by resolving
# `<its own WorkingDirectory>/user_data/plugins` — `crates/vike-studio-core/src/run.rs`'s
# `plugins_dir`, over `crates/vike-model/src/state_path.rs`'s `user_plugins_dir`. It reads NO
# variable for it. So the leaf below is not a preference: it is the half of a contract whose other
# half is a code path, and a Build that returns a green sha followed by a Run that answers
# `plugin artifact not found` is what a disagreement looks like.
#
# ⚠ **SUBSTITUTE THE SAME $R INTO THIS UNIT AND INTO `deploy/vike-backtest.service`.** The two
# templates carry DIFFERENT placeholder roots (`/srv/vike-<unit>` here,
# `/srv/vike-<unit>` there) and that is not a second location: the pair runs on ONE box out of
# ONE project root, the `sed` at the top of each file substitutes the same `$R`, and it is the
# shared LEAF that makes the two resolve to one directory afterwards. (This cited the hist-store
# line `deploy/vike-datahub.service` and `deploy/vike-backtest.service` shared as the precedent
# until 2026-09-26; the compute unit names no store since decision 0084, and the one-`$R` rule it
# argued now pairs those two by one settings directory and one node key pair instead.) Install
# this unit under a root of its OWN and the join is broken on that box, silently, until the first
# Run.
#
# `crates/vike-ops/tests/deploy_layout_gate.rs`'s `every_shipped_plugin_artifact_dir_is_one_spelling`
# holds the leaf equal on both sides — it reads THIS variable here and derives the reader's answer
# from that unit's `WorkingDirectory` there, which is the only comparison a text gate can make and
# is the one that was missing when the join first landed.
#
# ⚠ **A SPLIT ROOT IS NOT AVAILABLE IN A TRACKED UNIT, and this is where that bites.** Pointing
# this variable into another daemon's tree would need a `ReadWritePaths=` naming a path outside
# this unit's project folder, which `every_daemon_unit_is_one_project_folder` refuses — and under
# `ProtectSystem=strict` an ungranted write is `EROFS`, so the refusal is describing a physical
# fact rather than inventing a rule. One root, or an operator-owned departure this file cannot
# make for them.
#
# ⚠ **THE OWNERSHIP HAZARD, because the gate cannot see it.** `UMask=0077` at the bottom of this
# file means an artifact this daemon writes is readable by its own user and nobody else. Under a
# `--user` manager both daemons are that same user and it never bites. Under a SYSTEM install with
# a `User=` line, the two units MUST name the same account, or the compute daemon's `dlopen` fails
# with a permission error on a file that exists — which reads like a corrupt artifact and is not
# one.
Environment=VIKE_STRATEGY_BUILDER_OUT_DIR=/srv/vike-<unit>/user_data/plugins
Environment=VIKE_STRATEGY_BUILDER_RETAIN=5

# --- the source tree a rendered plugin's Cargo.toml resolves vike-model/vike-strategy/
# vike-indicators/vike-strategy-plugin against — REQUIRED, see the header note. NOT a sub-path of
# the project root above (a placeholder of its own, on purpose). Typically
# `<project root>/vendor/vike-strategy-source` — `scripts/fetch_release_tools.sh strategy-source`'s
# extraction target — once the project root above is substituted; a hand-maintained checkout works
# too, see the header note. Whatever it is, it is ALSO the third path on `BindReadOnlyPaths=` below:
# the jail shows this daemon nothing it does not bind.
# ⚠ **POINT IT AT `<project root>/vendor/vike-strategy-source` UNLESS YOU MEAN TO KEEP THE TREE
# IN STEP BY HAND.** That is the one path the automated deploy refreshes
# (`deploy/sbin/vike-trader-ci-deploy`'s `RUNTIME_TOOLS` directory row), stopping this unit, swapping
# the new release's tree in, and starting it again — because this binary REFUSES every Build
# against a tree stamped with any other commit. Any other path is left alone by the deploy (it may be
# your own checkout), so after every release it is yours to update, and until you do, every Build is
# refused — said on this unit's journal as a `Build REFUSED [source-version-mismatch]` line, and
# warned about by the deploy itself.
Environment=VIKE_STRATEGY_BUILDER_WORKSPACE_ROOT=/checkout/vike-trader-rust

# --- cargo's own home, INSIDE THE TOOLCHAIN TOOL DIRECTORY, NOT under settings ----------------
# ⚠ **Without this line no build on this unit can succeed — not "a build that needs to fetch a
# crate", EVERY build.** cargo takes an EXCLUSIVE file lock on `$CARGO_HOME/.package-cache` before
# essentially any dependency resolution, and `render::build_plugin` renders a scratch crate that
# has no `Cargo.lock` at all (it is content-addressed per source sha, created fresh) and passes
# neither `--offline` nor `--locked`, so every new sha resolves `toml` plus the whole `path`
# dependency closure and therefore takes that lock. Under `ProtectSystem=strict` a `$CARGO_HOME`
# not named in `ReadWritePaths=` is READ-ONLY, opening the lock file for writing is `EROFS`, and
# cargo fails the build. So the cache MUST be writable, and the write grant below names it.
#
# ⚠ **`--offline`/`--locked` were considered instead and do NOT fix it.** `--locked` requires a
# `Cargo.lock` the scratch crate does not have, so it turns every build into an error. `--offline`
# only stops cargo reaching the NETWORK — it still takes the package-cache lock, so a read-only
# `$CARGO_HOME` fails exactly the same way. A writable `$CARGO_HOME` is the fix; `--offline` is at
# most a later hardening on top of one, not a substitute.
#
# ⚠ **IT MOVED OUT OF `settings/state`, and that move is half the security fix.** It used to live at
# `settings/state/cargo-home`, which forced `ReadWritePaths=` to grant `settings/state` — the SAME
# directory where the trading node keeps its venue locks and a strategy's persisted state. A daemon
# that compiles a caller's arbitrary source had no business writing there. It now lives inside the
# toolchain's own tool directory as a WRITABLE DATA subdirectory (`bin/rust-toolchain/cargo-home`),
# versioned with the toolchain it caches for — a toolchain change is exactly the event that would
# want the cache rebuilt, so the two install and delete as one unit. The write grant below names
# ONLY this subdirectory, which reaches neither `bin/vike-backend` (this unit's own binary) nor the
# toolchain's `bin/cargo`/`bin/rustc` (its siblings), so "anything writable by this unit is data,
# never code" holds by containment — and `settings/` is not even mounted inside the jail below, so
# nothing under it is visible, let alone writable. An operator who wants the first build warm rather
# than cold seeds it once with `cp -a ~/.cargo/. <root>/bin/rust-toolchain/cargo-home/` before
# enabling the unit (or MOVES the old `settings/state/cargo-home` there, on a box that had one).
#
# ⚠ **It is also the ONE cargo configuration this daemon reads.** cargo's own children get a
# CLEARED environment (`render::CHILD_ENV_ALLOWLIST` — `PATH`, `CARGO_HOME`, `RUSTC`, `TMPDIR` and
# two rustup names, nothing else), so a proxy, a CA bundle or a registry mirror an operator needs
# goes in `<root>/bin/rust-toolchain/cargo-home/config.toml`, never in `.env` or an `Environment=`
# line — neither reaches cargo any more.
Environment=CARGO_HOME=/srv/vike-<unit>/bin/rust-toolchain/cargo-home

# --- the toolchain, NAMED OUTRIGHT rather than found through rustup ---------------------------
# ⚠ **These two lines name the toolchain's OWN `cargo`/`rustc` directly, never a rustup shim, and
# that is a MEASURED fix rather than a preference.** MEASURED on the CI box 2026-09-22 (systemd 255,
# `systemd-run --wait --pipe --collect` through the SYSTEM manager carrying this `[Service]`
# section's exact options as `-p` flags): a unit that reaches the toolchain through a rustup shim
# and no `RUSTUP_HOME` dies before cargo starts at all —
#
#   error: could not create home directory: '/root/.rustup': Read-only file system (os error 30)
#
# — because the shim wants to CREATE a home directory and `ProtectHome=yes`/`ProtectSystem=strict`
# leave it nowhere to. The other measured fix — `Environment=RUSTUP_HOME=…` plus a `PATH=` putting
# a `cargo/bin` first — also built, and is rejected for three reasons, none of them taste:
#   1. It makes the unit override PATH wholesale, and PATH is also how cargo finds the LINKER. A
#      unit that sets PATH to reach a compiler and quietly drops `cc` fails at the link step, which
#      surfaces as somebody else's error message.
#   2. It keeps a WRITE question alive. rustup's shim wants a home DIRECTORY; that pointing it at a
#      read-only one works is a property of rustup's current behaviour, not a guarantee.
#   3. It re-opens the home question this fix closed — a `RUSTUP_HOME` under `/home` is exactly what
#      `ProtectHome=yes` now hides, so the shim would be unreachable anyway.
# Naming the toolchain's own `cargo` and `rustc` needs no `RUSTUP_HOME`, no PATH override, and no
# rustup on the box at all — and it PINS the compiler a plugin is built with (see the header's
# toolchain-pin block), which is load-bearing when `deploy/vike-backtest.service` `dlopen`s the
# result into a running process. `crates/vike-strategy-builder/src/bin/vike-strategy-builder.rs`'s
# `main` reads `CARGO` out of the one environment sweep it owns (falling back to `"cargo"` off PATH
# — which, under a systemd unit's default PATH, is nothing at all); `RUSTC` is read by cargo itself.
# ⚠ Both paths are INSIDE the project (`$R/bin/rust-toolchain`), which the jail binds back
# read-only, so `ProtectHome=yes` hides the whole home directory without putting the compiler out of
# reach — the departure this unit used to carry (`ProtectHome=read-only`) is GONE.
Environment=CARGO=/srv/vike-<unit>/bin/rust-toolchain/bin/cargo
Environment=RUSTC=/srv/vike-<unit>/bin/rust-toolchain/bin/rustc

# ⚠ Set for structural consistency with every other unit in this directory
# (`crates/vike-ops/tests/deploy_layout_gate.rs`'s `every_daemon_unit_is_one_project_folder`
# requires every daemon to declare this), even though `vike-strategy-builder` does not read it —
# see this unit's header note on the credential-store gap. Inside the jail the directory it names
# does not exist; it is a label, and the deploy pre-flight reads it (from outside the jail) to find
# the settings tree to copy.
Environment=VIKE_SETTINGS_DIR=/srv/vike-<unit>/settings

# Operator tunables (NOT credentials — see the header note above) from a plain env file at the
# PROJECT ROOT; harmless if absent. Read by PID 1 before the jail exists. ⚠ They reach this daemon,
# NOT the compiler: `cargo` starts from a cleared environment (see the CARGO_HOME block).
EnvironmentFile=-/srv/vike-<unit>/.env

Restart=on-failure
RestartSec=5

# --- the write grant, NARROWED -----------------------------------------------------------------
# TWO targets on ONE line, and NEITHER is under `settings/` any more: the cargo cache inside the
# toolchain tool directory (`bin/rust-toolchain/cargo-home` — see the CARGO_HOME block for why it
# moved here out of `settings/state`) and the artifact directory this daemon exists to fill
# (`user_data/plugins`). `grant_covers` reaches neither `bin/vike-backend` (this unit's binary) nor
# `bin/rust-toolchain/bin/{cargo,rustc}` (the compiler it runs, a sibling of the granted cache), so
# everything writable by this unit is DATA, never code. They are the same two paths as the
# `BindPaths=` line below — the jail's read-write half — and
# `crates/vike-ops/tests/deploy_layout_gate.rs`'s `the_builder_reaches_only_what_it_builds` holds
# the two lines equal and fails a PR that puts a grant back under `settings/`.
ReadWritePaths=/srv/vike-<unit>/bin/rust-toolchain/cargo-home /srv/vike-<unit>/user_data/plugins

# --- THE FILESYSTEM JAIL — see the header's "WHAT THIS DAEMON CAN SEE" -------------------------
# An EMPTY, READ-ONLY tmpfs over every top-level directory a project root or another project's data
# lives under. `/srv` holds this template's placeholder root; `/mnt` holds the production box's real
# one, its CI runners' checkouts and credentials, and other projects' data volumes; `/opt` and
# `/media` hold nothing this daemon needs on any box. ⚠ YOUR root's top-level directory must be on
# this line — the header says how, and the gate checks it for this file's own root.
TemporaryFileSystem=/mnt:ro /srv:ro /opt:ro /media:ro
# …and what comes BACK, read-only: the binary this unit runs, the toolchain it compiles with, and
# the source tree the rendered crate's `path` dependencies resolve against. Nothing else of the
# project root — the gate refuses a bind that is not one of these three (or the two below).
BindReadOnlyPaths=/srv/vike-<unit>/bin/vike-backend /srv/vike-<unit>/bin/rust-toolchain /checkout/vike-trader-rust
# …and read-write: exactly the write grant above. `ReadWritePaths=` alone cannot do this any more —
# a path the tmpfs hid is not there to be made writable — so the bind is what makes each one EXIST
# inside the jail, and the grant is what keeps it writable under `ProtectSystem=strict`.
BindPaths=/srv/vike-<unit>/bin/rust-toolchain/cargo-home /srv/vike-<unit>/user_data/plugins
# ⚠ **There is no settings mask any more, and it is gone because it FAILED, not for tidiness.**
# This unit carried `InaccessiblePaths=<root>/settings` for a day. Under the tmpfs above that path
# does not exist inside the unit, and MEASURED on systemd 255 an `InaccessiblePaths=` entry naming a
# missing path fails the whole unit before the binary runs: `status=226/NAMESPACE — Failed to set
# up mount namespacing: …/settings: No such file or directory`. With a `-` prefix it starts, and
# masks nothing the jail had not already hidden. The gate refuses any bind under `settings/`, which
# is the property the mask was standing in for.
#
# What the jail CANNOT hide is `/etc` — a compiler needs its resolver config, CA bundle and password
# database — and the production box's sweep found two secret-bearing files there readable by this
# unit's user: a build-cache service credential under `/etc/vike`, and a firewall action's API
# token under `/etc/fail2ban`. Hidden by name; the `-` makes each a no-op on a box without it.
InaccessiblePaths=-/etc/vike -/etc/fail2ban

# --- syscall / capability hardening: the SAME measured set every other daemon unit here carries -
NoNewPrivileges=true
ProtectSystem=strict
# ⚠ **`ProtectHome=yes` — and this unit used to be the ONE DEPARTURE from that, which was the
# security hole this file closes.** Until 2026-09-26 this line read `read-only`, opening the
# operator's WHOLE home directory to a daemon that compiles a caller's arbitrary source. The stated
# reason was that the Rust toolchain lived under `~/.rustup`, and `ProtectHome=yes` (what every
# sibling unit carries) would put it outside this unit's mount namespace — which is exactly why
# `vike-backtest.service` cannot run `cargo` at all. But the home directory also holds `~/.ssh`,
# `~/.config/gh/hosts.yml` (a GitHub token) and, through the account that owns them, the settings
# database of venue credentials — so "read the toolchain" bought "read the operator's secrets", and
# a `build.rs` is arbitrary code (Decision 2). The fix is not to carve a hole into `/home`: it is to
# move the toolchain INTO the project (`bin/rust-toolchain`, see the header's toolchain block) so
# `/home`, `/root` and `/run/user` can be hidden ENTIRELY, exactly like every other daemon here.
# There is no departure left; this unit's job still needs a compiler, and the compiler is now a
# project artifact rather than a reason to open the home.
#
# ⚠ **MEASURED on the CI box 2026-09-22. This said "Not measured on a real box" and named a standing
# unknown; the unknown is closed, and it was a REAL DEFECT — this unit could not complete a single
# build.** Method: a cdylib crate compiled by `systemd-run --wait --pipe --collect` through the
# SYSTEM manager with every option of this `[Service]` section passed as `-p` flags, a
# `ReadWritePaths=` over the scratch directory, and BOTH the target directory and the `$CARGO_HOME`
# wiped before every row; systemd 255. Two crates were used and they agree: a dependency-free
# cdylib, and a 17-crate closure (serde+toml) whose build makes rustc DLOPEN a proc-macro `.so` —
# the code path `MemoryDenyWriteExecute=` actually governs.
#
# **The verdict: `SystemCallFilter=~@privileged` killed every build, and it is ONE syscall —
# `fchown`.** With no `User=` this unit runs as root, and cargo `fchown`s the files it creates while
# populating a COLD `$CARGO_HOME`. Denied, that is `SIGSYS`: `code=dumped/status=SYS`, ~150ms, no
# diagnostic of any kind, zero crates compiled. The `SystemCallFilter=fchown` line below re-allows
# exactly that one syscall and nothing else.
#
#   deny list                               MDWE   result (cold $CARGO_HOME, root)
#   --------------------------------------  -----  ------------------------------------
#   (no deny list)                          yes    BUILT
#   ~@privileged alone                      yes    killed: code=dumped/status=SYS
#   ~@chown alone                           yes    killed: code=dumped/status=SYS
#   ~fchown alone                           yes    killed: code=dumped/status=SYS
#   ~chown / ~fchownat / ~lchown alone      yes    BUILT   (so it is `fchown` ALONE)
#   the full list below, WITHOUT the fix    yes    killed: code=dumped/status=SYS, 0 crates
#   the full list below, WITH the fix       yes    BUILT, 17 crates, 3/3 repeats
#
# ⚠ **TWO CONDITIONS HIDE THIS, which is how it survived review and how a re-measurement can
# "disprove" it.** (1) A WARM `$CARGO_HOME` passes: cargo only chowns while CREATING that cache, so
# the second build on a box that ever got one build through is green, and so is any probe that
# forgets to wipe it. (2) A NON-ROOT service passes: with `-p User=the operator` the as-shipped filter
# BUILT, because cargo does not chown when it is not uid 0. So the defect bites exactly the shape
# this file ships — a system unit with no `User=` — on its FIRST build, which is the only build that
# decides whether the daemon works at all.
#
# ⚠ **A handed-down bisection attributed this to `MemoryDenyWriteExecute=yes` and a denied
# `@resources` being fatal TOGETHER. That did not reproduce here, in either crate, cold or warm.**
# Both were varied against both crates: with `fchown` allowed, `MemoryDenyWriteExecute=yes` WITH
# `@resources` denied BUILT all 17 crates, and dropping either one changed no row. So NEITHER is
# given up — `@resources` still denies `setrlimit`/`sched_setaffinity`/`ioprio_set` and friends,
# `MemoryDenyWriteExecute=` still denies W+X mappings, and the price of this fix is one syscall
# rather than a whole group or a whole exploit mitigation. If a future run does reproduce that pair,
# the variable to control FIRST is the one that moved every result here: whether `$CARGO_HOME` was
# cold, which is the state a fresh deployment is always in.
#
# ⚠ **The CARGO WRITE was part of that unknown and is no longer** — it is now known to be
# required on every build, and it is granted by the `CARGO_HOME` line above rather than left to
# be discovered. cargo locks `$CARGO_HOME/.package-cache` exclusively on essentially every resolve,
# and the scratch crate has no `Cargo.lock` to short-circuit one, so under a read-only `$CARGO_HOME`
# EVERY build fails — a daemon that could never do its one job, reported as
# `BuildError::Toolchain`/`BuildError::Io` to whoever asked for a plugin. The cache is now the
# project-local `bin/rust-toolchain/cargo-home`, and an operator may SEED it from `~/.cargo` before
# enabling the unit (see the `CARGO_HOME` block) — but the seed is a convenience, not a dependency.
#
# ⚠ **`ProtectHome=yes`, not `read-only`. See the long block above the write grant.** The toolchain
# lives in the project now, so nothing this daemon needs is under `/home`, `/root` or `/run/user` —
# and a daemon that compiles a caller's arbitrary source must not be able to read the operator's
# `~/.ssh`, GitHub token or dotfiles. This is the value every sibling unit carries; this unit
# stopped being the exception when the toolchain came indoors.
ProtectHome=yes
PrivateTmp=true
CapabilityBoundingSet=
AmbientCapabilities=
RestrictNamespaces=yes
# ⚠ **NO `AF_UNIX`, and this unit is the ONE daemon here that drops it.** A unix socket is how any
# local process reaches the system bus, and MEASURED on the production box 2026-09-26, unprivileged,
# from inside this unit: `busctl get-property … Service Environment` answered for all 243 loaded
# units — every sibling service's `Environment=`, readable by a `build.rs`. With `AF_UNIX` gone the
# same call fails (`Failed to connect to bus: Address family not supported by protocol`), and the
# daemon and a real build were re-measured unaffected: the listener is `AF_INET`, cargo's registry
# download and DNS use `AF_INET`/`AF_INET6` (the box resolves through `files dns`), and the journal
# stream is a descriptor systemd opens before the filter applies. A box whose resolver goes through
# a unix socket (`nss-resolve`, `nscd`) would lose DNS inside this unit — point `resolv.conf` at a
# resolver's address, or add `AF_UNIX` back and accept the bus.
RestrictAddressFamilies=AF_INET AF_INET6
RestrictSUIDSGID=yes
RestrictRealtime=yes
LockPersonality=yes
MemoryDenyWriteExecute=yes
SystemCallArchitectures=native
SystemCallFilter=@system-service
SystemCallFilter=~@privileged @resources @obsolete @mount @module @reboot @swap @raw-io @debug @clock @cpu-emulation
# ⚠ …and `fchown` BACK — THE ONE DEPARTURE from the sibling units' syscall set, measured in the
# block above. `@privileged` contains `@chown` contains `fchown`, and `fchown` is the single call a
# cold cargo build makes as root. A later `SystemCallFilter=` line with no `~` re-adds to the
# allow-list, so this is a one-syscall hole in the deny above rather than a relaxation of it:
# `chown`, `lchown` and `fchownat` stay denied, and each was measured individually as NOT needed.
# ⚠ **Do NOT delete this line to "align the hardening with the other units in this directory."** No
# other unit here runs a compiler, so no other unit needs it — and that is precisely the edit that
# would put this daemon back to zero successful builds with nothing in the journal but `status=SYS`.
# `crates/vike-ops/tests/deploy_layout_gate.rs`'s `the_builder_unit_can_actually_run_cargo` exists
# to make that edit fail in CI instead of on a box.
SystemCallFilter=fchown
ProtectKernelTunables=yes
ProtectKernelModules=yes
ProtectKernelLogs=yes
ProtectControlGroups=yes
ProtectClock=yes
ProtectHostname=yes
# ⚠ Hides OTHER users' processes. It does NOT hide a sibling daemon running as this unit's own user,
# whose `/proc/<pid>/environ` stays readable — the header's residual (3), and why `PrivatePIDs=`
# would be the next line here on a systemd new enough to have it (257+).
ProtectProc=invisible
ProcSubset=pid
UMask=0077

[Install]
# `default.target` is the `--user` spelling; a SYSTEM install — the one this file recommends, see
# the header — wants `multi-user.target`.
WantedBy=default.target
